WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.219
Attention is limited.

2
00:00:01.219 --> 00:00:09.342
Learners can only select and process a small amount of information at one time, especially when the material is new.

3
00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:17.466
Working memory is the mental workspace where people hold information while they compare, interpret, calculate, decide, or follow a procedure.

4
00:00:17.466 --> 00:00:28.433
When too much arrives at once, the learner may still be trying hard, but the mental workspace becomes crowded and the important relationship is harder to see.

5
00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:33.713
Cognitive load is the mental effort required to hold, process, and use information.

6
00:00:33.713 --> 00:00:38.181
Some load is necessary because the topic itself may be complex.

7
00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:39.806
Other load is avoidable.

8
00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:48.742
Unnecessary detail, cluttered visuals, inconsistent terminology, distracting side stories, and poorly sequenced explanations all consume attention without helping the learner build capability.

9
00:00:48.742 --> 00:00:52.398
The issue is not whether a course feels busy.

10
00:00:52.398 --> 00:00:58.896
The issue is whether the learner can focus on the decision, pattern, or action that matters.

11
00:00:58.896 --> 00:01:01.334
Good learning design manages load deliberately.

12
00:01:01.334 --> 00:01:13.113
It introduces concepts in a useful sequence, chunks material into meaningful units, removes visual and verbal clutter, and gives learners enough context to understand why the current idea matters.

13
00:01:13.113 --> 00:01:17.175
It also avoids dumping every exception into the first explanation.

14
00:01:17.175 --> 00:01:20.424
The aim is not to make learning shallow.

15
00:01:20.424 --> 00:01:28.954
The aim is to make the important structure visible so learners spend their effort on understanding instead of fighting the presentation.

16
00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:34.641
One practical move is to separate must-know structure from nice-to-know detail.

17
00:01:34.641 --> 00:01:43.577
Learners need the core pattern first: what it is, why it matters, how to recognize it, and what to do with it.

18
00:01:43.577 --> 00:01:48.857
Once that pattern is stable, exceptions and edge cases become easier to place.

19
00:01:48.857 --> 00:01:53.731
Without the core pattern, every detail competes for the same limited attention.
